+# Triton / vLLM Warm‑Pool & Batching Tuning Guide
+
+This guide explains how to run load tests and tune warm‑pool size, batching windows, and autoscale guard thresholds.
+
+1) Objectives & metrics
+ - Latency targets: p50/p95/p99 thresholds (e.g., p95 < 800ms)
+ - Throughput: requests/sec at acceptable latency
+ - GPU utilization: keep average < 85% to avoid OOM spikes
+ - Cost: minimize idle GPU time while satisfying latency SLO
+
+2) Set up
+ - Deploy Triton or vLLM on GPU nodes with nodeSelector node-role.kubernetes.io/gpu=true
+ - Deploy warm‑pool manager (services/serving/warm_pool_k8s.yaml)
+ - Ensure Prometheus scrapes model/server metrics and GPU exporter metrics
+ - Configure autoscale_guard to query Prometheus (services/autoscale/autoscale_guard.py)
+
+3) Load testing workflow
+ - Start with a small concurrency (2–4) and synthetic prompt payloads representative of real traffic
+ - Run:
+     python3 services/tests/triton_vllm_loadtest.py --url http://<model-proxy>/v1/generate --concurrency 4 --requests 200
+ - Increase concurrency and record p50/p95/p99 and GPU utilization
+ - Use metrics (Prometheus) to correlate queue length and batch sizes
+
+4) Tuning knobs
+ - Warm‑pool size: increase until cold-start latency disappears
+ - BATCH_WINDOW_SECS: shorter windows -> lower latency, but smaller batches -> higher GPU inefficiency
+ - MAX_BATCH_SIZE: cap to prevent memory blowups
+ - Model instance group count (Triton): scale to match throughput
+ - vLLM settings: sequence length, sampling parallelism, context window, kv cache settings
+
+5) Recommended iterative procedure
+ - Step A: Warm pool sizing
+   - Start with 1 worker per GPU; ramp up until tail latency stable
+ - Step B: Batch window tuning
+   - Increase batch window from 10ms to 100ms and watch batch size distribution; pick point with diminishing latency gains
+ - Step C: Autoscale thresholds
+   - Set autoscale_guard GPU_UTIL_THRESHOLD to 0.95; HPA scale based on queue length
+ - Step D: Safety valves
+   - Implement autoscale_guard ConfigMap to prevent further scale in runaway scenarios
+
+6) Example commands
+ - Run load test:
+    python3 services/tests/triton_vllm_loadtest.py --url http://proxy:8080/v1/generate --concurrency 8 --requests 2000
+ - Inspect Prometheus:
+    kubectl port-forward svc/aegis-prom-prometheus 9090:9090 -n aegis
+ - Apply warm‑pool recommended size (read ConfigMap):
+    kubectl get configmap aegis-warm-pool-config -n aegis -o yaml
+
+7) Acceptance criteria
+ - p95 latency at target load <= SLO
+ - GPU avg utilization <= 85% (no sustained >95%)
+ - No OOM events or model reload churn during test window
+ - Autoscale guard triggers and clears correctly on simulated runaway
+
+8) Troubleshooting
+ - If OOMs occur: reduce MAX_BATCH_SIZE, increase model instances, add node capacity
+ - If latency high despite warm pool: increase warm pool workers or reduce load per worker
+ - If cold starts observed: increase warm pool minimum replica count
+
